{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bc4692bbe4d514c3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 5\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9018 (GPU chargeback reporting for platform administrators)\n11\t\n12\tStrategy status: Refinement Done. Strategy priority: Major.\n13\t\n14\tTriage (Step 0): not below-threshold — the strategy spans two components\n15\t(Dashboard, Distributed Workloads), two teams, and multiple work-product types\n16\t(code implementation, cross-team validation, documentation authoring); HLRs\n17\tspan P0–P2. Not docs-only — Dashboard and Distributed Workloads are active or\n18\tpassive code-bearing components, only HLR-4 is documentation.\n19\t\n20\t## Step 1 parse notes and health warnings on source completeness\n21\t\n22\tThe strategy is a thin refined strategy. The following sections referenced by\n23\tthe decomposition method are absent and were reconstructed:\n24\t\n25\t- **Affected Components** is a bare list (Dashboard, Distributed Workloads) with\n26\t  no per-component change description or owning team. Owners were taken from\n27\t  `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/` (RHOAI Dashboard owns\n28\t  Dashboard; Distributed Workloads owns Distributed Workloads).\n29\t- **Impacted Teams table**, **Dependencies table**, formal **Acceptance\n30\t  Criteria** (Given/When/Then), and **Non-Functional Requirements** are absent.\n31\t  HLRs and the Out-of-Scope / Open-Questions sections carry the loadable\n32\t  content; acceptance criteria below were derived from HLR text.\n33\t- No Staff Engineer Input section — nothing to override.\n34\t\n35\t## Step 2 — component graph\n36\t\n37\t- **Dashboard** (RHOAI Dashboard team): active. All reporting surfaces — report\n38\t  computation, export/API, and the admin UI. Generates the implementation\n39\t  epics.\n40\t- **Distributed Workloads** (Distributed Workloads team): passive — the\n41\t  strategy requires no code change to it; the Kueue quota model\n42\t  (ClusterQueue/LocalQueue) is consumed as-is by the admin view. Because the\n43\t  validating team differs from the epic that touches it, the passive-component\n44\t  validation is a separate Implementation epic owned by the Distributed\n45\t  Workloads team (per the Step 2 exception).\n46\t- Both components exist in `.context/architecture-context/` and are Konflux\n47\t  onboarded — no onboarding chain (repo/license/konflux) is generated.\n48\t- The metering data source itself is owned by the in-flight RHAISTRAT-9990\n49\t  strategy and has no canonical RHAI component in `.context/rhai-components.txt`\n50\t  (the closest consumer-side canonical name, `Dashboard`, is used for the\n51\t  investigation that validates the contract). This mapping is noted here as\n52\t  non-obvious.\n53\t\n54\t## Step 3 — unknowns scan\n55\t\n56\tThe strategy's single flagged open question: the metering data schema\n57\tfinalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands. Decision rule: the answer changes\n58\twhat the reporting backend does (its data-access and computation design), so it\n59\tis a genuine unknown → **Investigation epic E001** validating the metering data\n60\tcontract. Its outcome is a true gate on the reporting backend\n61\t(`gate_failure_impact: rewrite` — an aggregation/adaptation layer is added if\n62\tthe contract lacks native per-project attribution or time-range granularity).\n63\t\n64\tNo conditional branch files were written: the bounded outcomes\n65\t(contract-consumable-as-is vs. contract-needs-adaptation) change the *scope* of\n66\tthe same downstream epic (rewrite), not which epics exist — the main plan\n67\tcovers the primary path and the deviation is recorded as the gate's fallback.\n68\t\n69\tNo other unknowns: HLR-2's \"stable API\" placement is API-surface discovery, an\n70\timplementation detail (AC + health warning below), not a structural unknown.\n71\tKueue quota semantics are corroborated by architecture context, not open.\n72\t\n73\t## Epic List\n74\t\n75\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n76\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n77\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 | Validate the GPU usage metering data contract for chargeback reporting | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n78\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | GPU chargeback reporting backend: per-project GPU-hour reports, CSV export, and stable API | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n79\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Dashboard admin view for GPU chargeback reports and Kueue quota comparison | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n80\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 | Validate Kueue quota attribution in the GPU chargeback admin view | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n81\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E005 | Document GPU chargeback report semantics and export integration | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n82\t\n83\t## Dependency DAG\n84\t\n85\t```mermaid\n86\tgraph TD\n87\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 Investigation: metering data contract] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 Reporting backend + export/API]\n88\t    E002 --> E003[RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 Admin view: reports vs Kueue quota]\n89\t    E003 --> E004[RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 DW validation of quota attribution]\n90\t    E002 --> E005[RHAISTRAT-9018-E005 Docs]\n91\t    E003 --> E005\n92\t    E004 --> E005\n93\t```\n94\t\n95\tCritical path: E001 → E002 → E003 → E004 → E005 (length 5).\n96\t\n97\t## DAG Justification\n98\t\n99\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n100\t|------|------|-----------|\n101\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate) | The metering data contract determines the reporting backend's data-access and computation design; outcome rewrites E002 scope if attribution/granularity is not natively supported. True gate recorded as `gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` on E002. |\n102\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | The admin UI builds against the reporting backend's report-data API; the backend produces the artifact the frontend consumes. |\n103\t| E003 → E004 | Step 2 passive-component exception | Distributed Workloads is passive (no code changes); the owning team's validation epic verifies the quota attribution implemented in the admin view, so it runs after the view exists. |\n104\t| E002 → E005, E003 → E005, E004 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation describes what was built; blocked by every Implementation epic. |\n105\t\n106\tNon-edges (parallelism rationale): none of the Rules 7/8/10 pairs apply — no\n107\trepo/license/konflux onboarding epics exist (both components are already\n108\tonboarded per architecture context). E001 is not an edge into E003/E004/E005\n109\tdirectly: its effect flows transitively through E002.\n110\t\n111\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n112\t\n113\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n114\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n115\t| HLR-1 (per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges) | P0 | E002, E003 | Backend computation + admin UI presentation of the same report surface. |\n116\t| HLR-2 (CSV export + stable API for finance tooling) | P1 | E002 (bundled) | Bundled into the P0 reporting backend: the export and API are delivery modes of the same report data structures and endpoints HLR-1 builds — the same per-project GPU-hour aggregation over time ranges serves the UI, the CSV, and the API. Splitting would build the same computation and API surface twice and add an artificial serialization edge between two epics owning one surface. Not independently deferrable. |\n117\t| HLR-3 (admin view: current-month consumption vs Kueue quota) | P1 | E003 (bundled), E004 | Bundled into the P0 admin view epic: the quota comparison renders in the same admin view/UI surface as the HLR-1 report tables (same screen, same per-project rows, same data-fetch path); a separate epic would rebuild the same view. E004 is the Distributed Workloads team's validation of the quota attribution (passive-component exception), not a second implementation. |\n118\t| HLR-4 (documentation of report semantics and export integration) | P2 | E005 | docs-authoring epic; priority derived from strategy `priority: Major` → P1 (docs-authoring priority exception — it does not push P0 upstream). |\n119\t\n120\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n121\t\n122\t## Health Warnings\n123\t\n124\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): Impacted Teams table,\n125\t   Dependencies table, formal Acceptance Criteria, and NFRs are absent; teams\n126\t   were inferred from architecture context and ACs derived from HLR text.\n127\t2. **Scope trap / architecture tension on HLR-2**: the Dashboard architecture\n128\t   context (`architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`) states the Dashboard exposes\n129\t   \"Kubernetes API only; no private component APIs\" and \"owns no data plane\",\n130\t   while HLR-2 requires a stable API for finance tooling. Where that API is\n131\t   served is unresolved by the strategy; per the unclear-passage rule this is\n132\t   API-surface discovery (implementation detail) captured as an AC on E002\n133\t   (documented, versioned API contract), but reviewers should confirm the\n134\t   placement decision does not spawn a new component epic later.\n135\t3. **External timing risk** (not an AC — externally controlled): the entire P0\n136\t   chain is gated on the RHAISTRAT-9990 metering schema finalizing. E001\n137\t   front-loads the risk by validating the contract early; the timing itself\n138\t   belongs to the other strategy and is recorded in E001/E002 Risks.\n139\t4. **Priority check**: no inversions found. E003 is P0 because it carries\n140\t   HLR-1's P0 UI surface (HLR-3's P1 is bundled, see matrix). E004 is P1 — it\n141\t   enables only HLR-3. E005 is P1 via the docs-authoring priority exception\n142\t   (Major → P1), exempt from inheritance.\n143\t\n144\t## Tiered Delivery\n145\t\n146\tEvaluated per Step 5.5 for the external dependency (RHAISTRAT-9990, in flight,\n147\tuncertain timing): the strategy cannot deliver independent user-facing partial\n148\tvalue without the metering data — every HLR-1/2/3 surface renders consumption\n149\tfigures computed from it, and the quota validation (E004) verifies the\n150\timplemented view. Therefore no Tier 1/Tier 2 split: linear dependencies with\n151\tthe external dependency blocking downstream, mitigated by the E001\n152\trisk-reduction investigation at the front of the chain.\n153\t\n154\t## Epic-size check\n155\t\n156\tAll epics are within the ~2-week guideline; no overage handling needed.\n157\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a85c2b9d083d1e57","agent_id":"a03579676fb753c67","session_id":"2c7f7242-e955-423b-a303-ec17bb0be124","uuid":"965ac5c2-64b8-4255-b8d0-2f7d364be4e4","timestamp":"2026-10-26T11:31:42.969Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bc4692bbe4d514c3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9018 decomposition"}
